PZ GIS Frontend — Usage Guide
Purpose

frontend.py provides a web interface for the pzgis GIS processor.

It allows you to:

    Select a country/source.

    Paste a GeoJSON area.

    Submit the area for GIS processing.

    Wait for pzgis to retrieve the relevant GIS data.

    Automatically download the resulting ZIP file.

The frontend is a FastAPI application. The browser UI is embedded directly inside frontend.py; there is no separate frontend application to build.

The processing flow is:

Browser
   │
   │ country + GeoJSON
   ▼
frontend.py
   │
   │ POST /api/process
   ▼
fetch_gis.py
   │
   ├── United States → US government GIS sources
   │
   └── Other countries → OpenStreetMap / Overpass
   │
   ▼
Generated GIS files
   │
   ▼
pzgis_output.zip
   │
   ▼
Browser download

1. Start the frontend

From the pzgis repository:

cd /path/to/pzgis

Start the application with:

uvicorn frontend:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000

The application will be available at:

http://localhost:8000

frontend.py is configured around port 8000. The __main__ block also starts Uvicorn on port 8000 with reload enabled, although the normal uvicorn frontend:app invocation is preferable for explicit operation.
2. Open the web interface

Open:

http://localhost:8000

The root endpoint:

GET /

returns the complete HTML interface.

The page is titled PZ GIS and provides:

    Country selection

    GeoJSON input

    Process GIS button

    Processing status

    Error/success messages

3. Select the country

The country selector currently contains:

    United States

    Japan

    Canada

    Mexico

    Brazil

    Germany

    France

    United Kingdom

    Australia

    New Zealand

    Other

The country selection determines which GIS source pzgis uses.
United States

Selecting:

United States

uses:

USA Structures
Census TIGERweb

Internally this is the us processing path.

The frontend describes this in the UI as:

Uses USA Structures and Census TIGERweb.

All other countries

The currently implemented non-US countries use:

OpenStreetMap / Overpass

This includes:

Japan
Canada
Mexico
Brazil
Germany
France
United Kingdom
Australia
New Zealand
Other

Internally these selections use:

--source osm

when calling fetch_gis.py.
Important

The country list should be understood as a source-selection mechanism, not as a collection of separate country-specific GIS implementations.

The code currently has only two actual processing paths:

United States → us
Everything else → osm

The explicit country list exists so that additional country-specific processors can be added later.
4. Provide the GeoJSON

Paste the GeoJSON describing the area to process into the large GeoJSON text box.

The expected input is a JSON object containing a type property.

For example:

{
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "features": [
    ...
  ]
}

The UI specifically describes this field as:

Paste the GeoJSON containing the area you want to process.

The GeoJSON is the area to query, rather than the buildings/roads output. Those GIS layers are generated by fetch_gis.py.
5. Submit the request

Click:

Process GIS

The browser first validates the contents locally.

It performs:

JSON.parse(...)

against the text in the GeoJSON box.

If the text is not valid JSON, the browser displays:

The GeoJSON is not valid JSON.

If the JSON is valid but has no type property, it displays:

The GeoJSON does not contain a 'type' property.

No request is sent to the server in either case.
6. What the browser sends

After validation, the browser sends:

POST /api/process

with:

Content-Type: application/json

The request body is:

{
  "country": "United States",
  "geojson": {
    "type": "FeatureCollection",
    "features": []
  }
}

The exact GeoJSON supplied by the user is placed inside the geojson property.
7. Server-side processing

frontend.py receives the request using the GISRequest model:

class GISRequest(BaseModel):
    country: str
    geojson: dict

The server then performs several validations.
Processor validation

It checks that:

fetch_gis.py

exists alongside frontend.py.

If it does not, the API returns HTTP 500.
Country validation

The supplied country must exist in the COUNTRY_SOURCES mapping.

Otherwise:

HTTP 400
Unsupported country: <country>

is returned.
GeoJSON validation

The server verifies that:

    the GeoJSON is a JSON object

    it contains a type property

Invalid input produces HTTP 400.
8. Temporary processing directory

Each request receives a unique temporary working directory.

The directory is based on a generated UUID and has a name similar to:

/tmp/pzgis_<job-id>/

Inside that directory, the submitted GeoJSON is saved as:

area.geojson

The server writes the JSON with indentation before passing it to the GIS processor.

The important consequence is that the user does not need to create area.geojson manually when using the web frontend.
9. fetch_gis.py execution

The frontend launches the existing GIS processor as a subprocess.

The base command is effectively:

python3 fetch_gis.py area.geojson <working-directory>

For the United States:

python3 fetch_gis.py \
    area.geojson \
    <working-directory>

For non-US sources:

python3 fetch_gis.py \
    area.geojson \
    <working-directory> \
    --source osm

The command is executed with the pzgis repository as its working directory.
10. Processing timeout

The frontend allows the GIS subprocess to run for a maximum of:

15 minutes

The timeout is implemented as:

timeout=15 * 60

If the processor runs longer than 15 minutes, it is stopped and the API returns HTTP 504:

pzgis took longer than 15 minutes and was stopped.

The browser displays this as a processing failure.
11. Generated GIS files

After fetch_gis.py finishes successfully, the frontend looks in the temporary working directory for these possible files:

buildings.geojson
roads.geojson
water.geojson
landuse.geojson

Only files that actually exist are included.

Therefore, the output does not require all four layers to be present.

If none of these files were produced, the frontend returns HTTP 500 with:

pzgis completed but produced no GIS files.

The processor's stdout and stderr are also included in the error response.
12. Output ZIP

Once one or more GIS files have been produced, the frontend creates:

pzgis_output.zip

The ZIP contains:

area.geojson

plus every generated GIS layer that exists.

For example:

pzgis_output.zip
├── area.geojson
├── buildings.geojson
├── roads.geojson
├── water.geojson
└── landuse.geojson

The actual contents depend on what fetch_gis.py generated for the selected source and area.
13. Processing log

The ZIP also contains:

pzgis.log

This is generated from the processor's:

stdout

and:

stderr

The log is structured approximately as:

<processor stdout>


===== STDERR =====


<processor stderr>

This makes pzgis.log the primary artifact to inspect when trying to understand what happened during a completed processing job.
14. Browser download

The server responds with the ZIP as an HTTP file response.

The browser reads the response as a binary blob and automatically creates a download.

The filename is based on the selected country:

pzgis-<country>.zip

Spaces are replaced with underscores.

Examples:

pzgis-United_States.zip
pzgis-Japan.zip
pzgis-United_Kingdom.zip
pzgis-New_Zealand.zip

The browser downloads the file automatically when processing succeeds.
